draftRequest.text:
Implement these Draft Cards in {where}, matching each one's description, content and the existing connections, and keeping the Card ids. Drafts have no ports: their wire ends use port "". In the same project_save as each implementation, send SetProject rebinding every incoming wire to a real input and every outgoing wire to a real output; also rebind boundary endpoints. State each port explicitly even when only one is possible. Ends on remaining Drafts stay empty. Each Card's _meta.json keeps what it has word for word and gets what is missing with the save that implements it: with keep_meta, fill_description if it has none and fill_context from what the user asked in this conversation and what the description and content say, stating nothing they did not say (see the rules). Use a built-in Card where it is enough (data already in the project is handed on by an input Card with a File output; see the rules), otherwise a code Card (language, default.nix, sources and _outputs.fbs). The rules hold what Python, C++ and bash Cards need, so start writing; read a reference only for what they do not cover.
Write and save all of them first, upstream first. Then check them once with project_validate, fix what it reports, run the workflow once from its trigger Card and confirm each Card's result with run_record. Run earlier only when what you write next needs something you can learn only from a run. At the end, report what each Card does and what the run produced.
{list}

commentRequest.text:
The user left comments on these Cards in {where}; do what they ask. By default change the Card a comment is on, keeping its id and wiring. When a comment asks for it, add Cards or split the Card: SetProject for the Cards and connections in the same save; the host supplies new Card positions. A Draft among them is implemented as the rules say, following its comments. Read each Card first. The rules hold what Python, C++ and bash Cards need; read a reference only for what they do not cover.
The save that does what a comment asks also resolves it: PutDefinition with "meta_json" holding the Card's _meta.json, with that comment's "resolved": true and a "resolution" of one line on what was done, in the user's language (see the rules). Tsugumi records with it which Cards that save changed, so that the user can follow a comment to its Cards. Write and save them all first, check them once with project_validate, then run what changed once and confirm each result with run_record; when a result is not what its comment asks, go on until it is, and correct the resolution. A comment you cannot do stays open: say why in your report. At the end, report what you did for each comment and what the run produced.
{list}
